    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d351">

                <pb facs="0773" n="723" sameAs="frus:frus1919Parisv12/pg723"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Paris Peace Conf. 181.9202/251: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">General Bandholtz to
                        the Commission to Negotiate Peace</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Budapest</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-11-18">November 18, 1919</date>.<lb/>[Received November
                        18—7:30 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>207. Polk from Bandholtz. Replying to your 105.<anchor n="70" xml:id="d351-f1"/>
                    The facts did not justify great concern over reported arrests. Horthy’s conduct
                    throughout has been excellent. Present indications are that Friedrich within 24
                    hours will be replaced by Apponyi or somebody else. Political outlook now
                    encouraging. Reports of Roumanian progress in evacuation not so good. They have
                    just seized entire Hungarian tobacco supply at Szolnok and it is reported
                    excesses are being committed. They have left no liaison officer but one of my
                    officers will be at their headquarters on 19th and report on conditions. Colonel
                    Loree should arrive Paris Thursday with memorandum and so you can question him
                    on all details prior to your departure.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bandholtz</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
